+
+## Chapter 10 retention amendment
+
+Runtime policy is `RetentionPolicy{DiagnosticMaxRecords: 200, LogMode: "full"}`
+by default. A nil `Config.Retention` uses that default; a supplied policy is
+validated and copied. `Agent.RetentionPolicy` / `SetRetentionPolicy` provide
+an independent, concurrency-safe policy seam. Negative limits and unknown modes
+are errors. Explicit zero disables diagnostic trimming, not saved-history retention.
+The archive's representation never replaces the current runtime policy.
+
+CLI and GUI read these keys from the existing `cr/settings.json`:
+
+```json
+{"diagnostic_max_records": 200, "log_mode": "full"}
+```
+
+The GUI's Chapter 8 partial-update/persist/reconnect path includes both fields.
+Missing keys use defaults; unrelated partial updates preserve explicit zero.
+Changes publish to runtime only after successful settings persistence and take
+effect at the next retention boundary. `current_settings` and `settings_changed`
+include both values. CLI reads the same settings on its next startup.
+
+`full` writes all available original canonical events. `dialogue_only` writes
+ordered `{source_seq, role, text}` records derived from human/assistant text,
+with no tool calls/results, opaque/provider-private payloads, metadata or traces
+in that projection. Carried dialogue records survive subsequent saves and mode
+switches. Original events are never fabricated from dialogue. The full current
+snapshot remains authoritative, including replay-private state; dialogue-only
+is **not** a private-data erasure guarantee. The writer folds the available
+canonical tail into the new snapshot before omitting the covered originals.
+A loaded post-anchor canonical tail remains intact and applies exactly once.
+In-memory History is not filtered by either saved-log mode.
+
+Snapshotless replay requires a canonical stream starting at sequence 1 without
+missing sequence numbers (or an empty stream at the initial frontier). A high
+`as_of` alone does not prove missing events: it also controls future sequence
+allocation. Snapshot-backed trimmed archives remain valid. Available prior
+`dialogue_log` is preserved when returning to full mode, but missing original
+facts cannot be recovered; such an archive cannot be used as a complete-log replay.
+
+Clients own `DiagnosticLog` writers for CLI `api.log` and GUI `debug.log`,
+`api.log`, `gui.log`. Each append is one JSON-framed record, even if its payload
+contains multiple lines. Cleanup holds the same mutex as appends, retains the
+newest whole records in order, syncs a sibling temporary replacement, atomically
+renames it, and switches the live descriptor before accepting more writes.
+Earlier application timestamp/API-prefix record starts are recognized; unknown
+legacy text remains an indivisible record rather than being guessed apart.
+External writers, concurrent application instances sharing one file, hostile
+filesystem changes, and power-loss durability of directory entries are not
+supported guarantees. Diagnostic framing is not provider-payload sanitization.
+
+Lifecycle boundaries are startup **after** successful recovery (or successful
+establishment of a first recovery save), and normal exit **after** successful
+save and admitted-work completion. Explicit `Save` is not an additional cleanup
+boundary. Load/save failures do not invoke cleanup. Trim failures are returned
+without invalidating the recovery save or closing the still-live old descriptor.
+There is no continuous disk cap. Canonical History, Context, provider projection,
+and recovery tails are never trimmed by the diagnostic limit. The callback in
+`Config.TrimDiagnostics` supplies only the actual logs owned by that application;
+core does not search for files or open/truncate unused streams.
+
+Chapter 14 handoff/journal retention is deliberately deferred. No handoff,
+background archival job, provider/browser run, or legacy campaign is implemented
+by this delta. Focused tests are `go test ./... -run '^TestRetention'` in each
+module, in addition to the existing complete offline suites.
